.TH LINUX-BACKUP 8 "November 2026" "linux-backup 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
linux-backup \- scheduled, logged and verified backups of /etc and /home
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linux-backup
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linux-backup
controls a cron-driven backup. Each run archives the configured directories with
.BR tar (1)
into a date-stamped, gzip-compressed file in the backup directory, logs every step,
verifies the archive by listing it, sets it to mode 600, and removes archives older
than the retention period.
.PP
Settings live in
.IR /etc/linux-backup.conf .
The cron entry in
.I /etc/cron.d/linux-backup
is generated from that file and should not be edited by hand.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show whether scheduled backups are enabled, the schedule, the retention period,
whether crond is running, how many archives exist and the last logged result.
.TP
.B enable
Turn scheduled backups on.
.TP
.B disable
Turn scheduled backups off. Existing archives are kept. A backup that is already
running finishes normally.
.TP
.B schedule
Print the current schedule.
.TP
.BI "schedule """ "minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week" """"
Change the schedule. Fields are numeric: numbers, lists (1,15), ranges (1\-5) and steps (*/2, 1\-10/3).
Quote the expression so the shell does not expand the stars.
A schedule that fires more than once an hour is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given, because every run writes a full archive.
.TP
.BI "schedule \-\-daily " HH:MM
Run once a day at the given 24-hour time.
.TP
.B run
Run a backup now, whatever the schedule says.
.TP
.B list
List the archives.
.TP
.B apply
Rebuild the cron entry from the configuration file, for example after editing it by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/linux-backup.conf
Configuration: ENABLED, SCHEDULE, SOURCES, BACKUP_DIR, RETENTION_DAYS, LOG_FILE.
.TP
.I /etc/cron.d/linux-backup
Generated cron entry.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/linux-backup/backup.sh
The backup script.
.TP
.I /backup
Default archive directory, mode 700.
.TP
.I /var/log/backup.log
Log of every run.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo linux-backup status
sudo linux-backup disable
sudo linux-backup enable
sudo linux-backup schedule "30 3 * * *"
sudo linux-backup schedule \-\-daily 03:30
sudo linux-backup schedule "0 2 * * 1\-5"
sudo linux-backup run
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error, 2 for an unknown command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crontab (5),
.BR cron (8),
.BR tar (1),
.BR find (1)
